artifact.self_contained_problem:
In ultra-relativistic heavy-ion collisions, a "centrality class" is an equivalence class of events grouped by collision geometry. Theoretical models typically classify events by the impact parameter b (the transverse distance between the centers of the two colliding nuclei) or by initial-state observables such as the number of participating nucleons N_part or the number of produced gluons N_g at proper time τ_0. Experimental analyses, in contrast, classify events by final-state observables, most commonly the charged-particle multiplicity dN_ch/dη or the transverse energy dE_T/dη at midrapidity, after the full dynamical evolution of the system.

Let P(b) denote the geometric probability density of impact parameters. Let N_g(b) be a (model-dependent) initial gluon number, let s_0(b,x_⊥) denote an initial entropy density profile produced at τ_0, and let the subsequent evolution be described by relativistic viscous hydrodynamics with shear viscosity over entropy density η/s and bulk viscosity ζ/s, followed by hadronic freeze-out yielding a final charged multiplicity N_ch. Because the evolution is dissipative, the total entropy S(τ) = ∫ s(τ,x) d^3x is not conserved: S(τ_f) > S(τ_0).

Formulate a procedure for defining and matching centrality classes between theory and experiment that satisfies all of the following requirements:

(a) The mapping b ↦ N_g(b) ↦ S(τ_0;b) ↦ S(τ_f;b) ↦ N_ch(b) is specified explicitly, including its event-by-event fluctuations, and the cumulative distribution of the chosen classifier on the theory side is matched to the cumulative distribution of the experimental classifier;

(b) The amount of dissipative entropy production during hydrodynamic evolution is quantified and incorporated, so that classifying by initial entropy or by final multiplicity yields consistent (and quantitatively related) centrality bins;

(c) Any assumed proportionality between b, N_part, N_g, S(τ_0), and N_ch is verified rather than assumed, with errors controlled;

(d) The resulting comparison between computed and measured observables (e.g., anisotropic flow coefficients v_n) is sufficiently sensitive to extract the QGP transport coefficients η/s and ζ/s with quantifiable systematic uncertainty arising from the centrality definition itself.

Provide such a matching prescription, prove that it minimizes the systematic bias on the extracted transport coefficients across plausible choices of initial-condition model, and characterize the residual uncertainty as a function of centrality.
